<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nama people</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Namaqua</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Nama are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khoekhoe">Khoekhoe</a> people of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">Namibia</a> and the north-western Cape of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, traditionally pastoralists who speak Khoekhoegowab, a click language. In the 19th century many Nama communities lived under kapteins, often alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oorlam">Oorlam</a> groups and Christian missions. Their rising against German rule in 1904–1908 was crushed with genocidal violence, and thousands died in German camps.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nama-people.jpg" alt="Nama people" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Greg Willis from Denver, CO, usa · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nama_man_greeting_us_(3693359757).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Nama, in older sources also called Namaqua, are the largest group of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khoekhoe">Khoekhoe</a> peoples. They live in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">Namibia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/botswana">Botswana</a>, many of them in central Namibia and others in Namaqualand, which straddles the Namibian border with South Africa. They traditionally speak the Nama language of the Khoe-Kwadi family, though many also speak <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaans">Afrikaans</a>. They originally lived on the banks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orange-river">Orange River</a> in southern Namibia and northern South Africa. The name Namaqua adds the Khoekhoe suffix -qua, meaning place of, to the people's name. Under pressure from the expanding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a> some Khoekhoe, including Nama, crossed the Orange River into what became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-south-west-africa">German South West Africa</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jager-afrikaner">Jager Afrikaner</a> led a Nama community from Little Namaqualand to found a permanent settlement north of the Garib, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orange-river">Orange River</a>, in 1795. In 1823-24 his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jonker-afrikaner">Jonker Afrikaner</a> led part of the Oorlam Afrikaner community to the area of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/windhoek">Windhoek</a>, where they built a society centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, trade and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. Hendrik Witbooi took over the leadership of the Witbooi Nama from his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a> in 1884, led his people north in search of new land and clashed with the Herero under Chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharero">Maharero</a>, who had made a protection agreement with the new German colonial administration.</p><p>In April 1893 the German Landeshauptmann Curt von François attacked Witbooi's settlement at Hoornkrans with 214 soldiers, and German troops killed nearly one hundred Nama women and children in their sleep. After a year of skirmishing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodor-leutwein">Theodor Leutwein</a>, with enlarged forces, defeated the Nama, who had run out of ammunition, and forced Witbooi to sign a protection treaty. From 1904 to 1908 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> waged war on the Nama and Herero, a war now called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herero-and-nama-genocide">Herero and Nama genocide</a>: herds were confiscated, people were driven into the desert or interned in camps, as on Shark Island, and forced to work on railways and in the diamond fields. Witbooi was killed in battle on 29 October 1905.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Nama practise communal land ownership. Music, poetry and storytelling are important, and they are known for leatherwork, skin karosses and mats, reed flutes, jewellery and clay pots. The traditional dwelling is the haru oms, or matjieshuis, a domed hut of reed mats on a frame of sticks, woven by women and easy to take down when grazing becomes scarce. Camps once held five to thirty huts with their doors facing the centre. Most Nama in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">Namibia</a> today are Christian, and the women's formal dress, long and flowing, developed from the style of the missionaries of the 1800s. In December 2002 ancestral lands in the Richtersveld, including its national park, were returned to community ownership.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nama people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Bondelswarts, in <b>Nama</b> !Gami-ǂnun, were a Khoekhoe-speaking Nama group living north of the Orange River.</blockquote><a href="/state/bondelswarts">Bondelswarts</a></li><li><blockquote>The Herero rising of 1904 and the <b>Nama</b> war under Hendrik Witbooi were crushed by General Lothar von Trotha; tens of thousands died in the desert and in concentration camps such as Shark Island.</blockquote><a href="/state/german-south-west-africa">German South West Africa</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1823 his son Jonker Afrikaner moved north at the invitation of the <b>Nama</b> to help them against the Herero, and by about 1840 he had settled at Windhoek, which he made his capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/orlam-afrikaner-state">Orlam Afrikaner State</a></li><li><blockquote>The Basters, descendants of Cape Dutch settlers and Khoekhoe women, left the northern Cape in 1868 and under Kaptein Hermanus van Wyk settled at Rehoboth, south of Windhoek, in 1870, acquiring land from the Swartbooi <b>Nama</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/rehoboth-basters">Rehoboth Basters</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1863 Kido Witbooi led them north from Pella to Gibeon in central southern Namibia, where a Rhenish mission was established and they became one of the leading <b>Nama</b> communities.</blockquote><a href="/state/witbooi-nama">Witbooi Nama</a></li></ul></section>
